diagnosis_text:
No fault found. The Location C gateway (router4) is up and forwarding. Evidence:
- From PC11 (192.168.200.11), the default route points to 192.168.200.1 via eth0, and router4’s LAN interface eth0 is up at 192.168.200.1/24.
- PC11→router4 pings succeed with 0% loss and sub‑millisecond RTT, confirming the gateway is answering.
- Inter‑site reachability works: PC11 and other Location C PCs can ping 172.16.10.4 (remote site) successfully, showing L3 forwarding beyond the local LAN.
- On router4, FRR/RIP is running (UDP/520 open; vty ports listening), and the routing table contains a healthy mix of connected, default (via 172.25.1.2 on eth5), and RIP-learned routes (via 192.168.15.1 on eth4 and 172.25.1.2 on eth5). Router5 reciprocally has routes pointing back to 192.168.200.0/24 via 192.168.15.2, confirming adjacency.
Mechanism: Because the gateway interface and dynamic routing are up, any claim of “router down” would present as unreachable gateway and no inter‑site routes, which is not observed. Localization: Location C’s router4 eth0 (192.168.200.1), uplinks eth4 (192.168.15.2 to router5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1 to router3) are operational.
Root cause: Not reproducible; no router outage detected.
Corrective fix: None required on the network. If users still see issues, check individual hosts (NIC state/DHCP/DNS), or transient LAN issues; consider clearing ARP or restarting host NICs. Monitor router4 but no action is needed now. 
